From: price@price.demon.co.uk (Michael Clive Price)
Subject: "Experiment of interest to AI and quantum computer designers"

James A. Donald:

> The interesting thing about a quantum computer is that in
> principle it can compute some things that require non
> polynomial time (effectively infinite time for interesting
> problems) for a classical computer.

Not so and I'll wager 5,000 UK pounds, 1-1, on that.

My claim is that a quantum computer, as defined in the Deutsch
references you gave (which I am familiar with), does not permit
unbounded or non-polynomial computation.  As arbitrator I suggest
Deutsch (subject to his approval).  Any reasonable terms accepted.

There are two main reasons:
1)   the quantum computer (which carries out classical computations in
     parallel in different quantum states) has a nasty habit of erroring
     as the parallelism is pumped up, which more than wipes any
     advantages to this method.  eg if it's carrying out a thousand
     calculations in parallel then it is only able to successfully
     complete in less than 1 run in a thousand and you have to rerun the
     computer a 1000 times (on average) before you get an answer out. 
     ie the _mean_ time to completion remains bounded from below by the
     classical time.  The erroring is a intrinsic to the design and not
     something that can be removed with greater fidelity.
2)   the range of problems the above approach works for (subject to the
     above restriction) becomes more and more restricted as the
     parallelism is jacked up.

> James A. Donald

