Date: Thu, 23 Sep 93 12:31:39 PDT
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: Archiving the List, Policies, etc.

(I've decided to cc: the Extropians list on this message, as it does
not contain quoted e-mail from Ray or Harry, and it's time this issue
get wider discussion. Sorry if anyone is offended by the candor.)


Ray, Harry,

I feel strongly that the debate about the future of the List, the
archiving of traffic, and payment policies belongs on the List proper.
I appreciate being copied on your debate about the future of the list,
and the archiving issue, but I feel the whole List needs to be kept
informed as well.

Yes, it may bug some folks, but any thread runs this risk.

Truly secret information that you wish to keep to yourselves--such as
financial status, issues--is another matter, but then I should not be
copied either.

I would like to participate in the debate, but I'd rather do it
publically. For example, I think most of us assumed that someone with
a more direct, "non-mail," access to the List (e.g., on the gnu or
panix machines) was archiving the list traffic as a matter of course.

That no one is known to be archiving the List, except in a
catch-as-catch-can, informal way, is very disturbing news.

That this has _not_ been happening may come as surprising news to many
on the List. And telling folks this may actually spur some volunteers
with good access to start archiving everything. (The indisputable
work needed to do this could be lessened by someone setting up a
_second_ mail address that is intended to subscribe to all messages
and go into an archive, with no work on their part except normal
backups. This person could get some recognition by being called "List
Archivist," or somesuch.)

I throw out 80% or more of the List traffic, as I mentioned, and I
suspect others do as well. Reconstructing the List archives from
fragments--what we may call the "Jurassic Park" strategy--could be
very time-consuming. Just getting the tens of megabytes held (my
Extropian archives are fragmentary--10% of the traffic--and yet fill
about 20-40 MB) to a central site and then merging and purging will be
a real chore...forget doing it by _hand_, with zillions of requests of
the sort, "Does anyone have the March 23, 1992 message from Perry
Metzger on how he became a Randroid?".

Wouldn't it be deliciously ironic if in decades hence there is no
record of the debate here just because nobody archived the list? Boy,
some Extropians we are!

(I'd volunteer, but my permanent storage of mail is via Eudora over a
dial-up line. I try to discard as much mail as possible in my on-line
reader before downloading. The ideal situation is someone with a Unix
machine, a fast connection, who can store the traffic transparently
and cheaply, on DATs or whatever that are already in use at his or her
site.)

The policies about "access to messages when not subscribed" complicate
matters, as no one, for example, has any financial or reputation
incentive to make their archives available at an ftp site, in a
WWW-WAIS system, or even to compare archives with others. (This would
clearly "violate" the list rules unless it was confirmed--how?--that
all participants were legally subcribed for all the times
covered....very confusing, very counterproductive to getting archives
built.)

I urge this debate go on the List proper. (Note: and I have decided to
post these comments to the List. Some may complain, but I see no basis
for this. Debating the nature of the List, the archiving ideas, etc.,
is *at least* as important as most of the stuff that flows onto the
List.)

Cheers,

-Tim May


