Date: Thu, 23 Sep 93 14:24:54 -0700
From: jamie@netcom.com (Jamie Dinkelacker)
Subject: Crypto-Anarchy & Libertarian RealPolitik

Ref  X-Message-Number: #93-9-1279

My thanks to both Tim May and Nick Szabo regarding Crypto Anarchy and
Nick's recent RealPolitik post.

As some Extropians already know, I've worked with both electronic
marketplaces and the broadcast media. In both of these environments,
information and data distribution are used directly to make money. These
experiences, focused and framed by Tim's cogent rhetoric, lead me to expect
"Black Net" as a nearly inevitable outcome of electronic marketplaces,
particularly one in which any subscriber can post, buy, broker or agent for
self or others. The heralded protection of using "true names" will become
increasingly difficult to enforce, even if actual/absolute identifiers to
birth or immigration could be trailed -- doubtful at best. I think Tim is
accurate in his expectation that a "democratic" marketplace opens the
floodgates for corporate workers to make BigBucks by selling "corporate
secrets." Un-true names (cypher aliases?), digicash, anonymous remailers,
... , all point in this direction. Especially if encrypted information is
listed in a market, and a single key is sold to the highest bidder. What's
to stop it? An honor code? Boyscout oath? Having the marketplace owners vet
every message? Hardly.

I get the dubious pleasure of reviewing numerous business plans for
startups seeking (venture) funding. For those in the content generation or
distribution domains, "market research info" is often a revenue line,
albeit several years down the road. In other words, startup founders
recognize the value of selling information about their
customers/clients/users to others. Nick readily points this out in his
phrasing of "Banks would much rather build marketing dossiers for vendors
than protect the privacy of their customers." Bingo. Plus, from the bank's
conservative perspective, of course they can be trusted ... after all,
they're the bank (or store, or agency, etc.). I'm not so sanguine about
their responsibility on my behalf -- or yours -- or anybodys except their
own. 

The lesson I took from _Shockwave Rider_ (18 years ago?!!) was the
emergence of social dynamics in which most -- not all -- political power
will be derived from an increased emphasis on the relative and differential
access to information between the individual and the bureaucracy (state,
corporate or otherwise) in contrast to current political control based on
propinquity and physical property as indicators of tangible wealth. This
emergence is hastened by the net, crypto, and the growing "information"
economy. As one boundary value, individual communications, and spontanteous
audiences/groups, could become impenetrable to the corporatnoids/statists.
As another boundary value, to paraphrase the Firesign Theater, "ask that
cop knocking on your back door" (knock knock). Recent supoenas raise this
